== Title decisions ==

* '''[[Utility]] → [[Utility (decision theory)]]''' because “utility” has durable meanings in software, public services, infrastructure, economics, philosophy, and ordinary language. This page is specifically the formal preference/value representation used in decision theory and game theory.
* [[Coordination problem]] remains distinct from [[Collective action]]: coordination can fail from incompatible expectations even when interests broadly align, while collective-action problems can involve incentives to defect or free-ride.
* [[Public good]] remains distinct from [[Commons]]: a pure public good is classically non-rival and non-excludable, while many commons are common-pool resources that are rival/subtractable but difficult to exclude from.
* [[Governance]] remains unqualified and public: it covers institutional decision processes across states, firms, communities, platforms, associations, and decentralized systems.
* [[Sybil attack]] remains the established distributed-systems/security term and is not renamed to a blockchain-specific title.

== Scope corrections from the provisional queue ==

The earlier provisional Wave 10 queue listed [[Preference]] and [[Risk]]. They are retained as future coverage signals rather than created here because:

* preference is a broader psychological/philosophical/economic concept that needs its own cross-domain capture;
* risk spans decision theory, statistics, engineering, finance, medicine, and safety and should not be defined only through this wave.

The refined wave adds [[Coordination problem]], [[Public good]], [[Commons]], [[Principal–agent problem]], [[Governance]], [[Reputation system]], and [[Sybil attack]] because they create the public conceptual bridge from individual choice to institutions and decentralized collective systems.

== Domain decision ==

No new controlled Domain is introduced.

Wave 10 uses existing Domains:

* Philosophy
* Mathematics
* Systems science
* Computer science

Economics, political science, and social science remain broader future Domain-scope questions rather than being invented to satisfy this wave.

