coordination problem ≠ collective-action problem by identity
collective action ≠ coordination problem
public good ≠ government-provided good
commons ≠ public good
principal–agent problem ≠ employer–employee relationship only
governance ≠ government
reputation ≠ identity
Sybil attack ≠ pseudonymity
coordination problem ≠ collective-action problem
public good ≠ government-provided good
commons ≠ open access by definition
commons ≠ inevitable tragedy
governance ≠ government
reputation ≠ trust
Sybil attack ≠ pseudonymity
</pre>

== Source families ==

* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy — decision theory, expected utility, game theory, public goods, and free-rider/collective-action boundaries.
* von Neumann & Morgenstern; Savage; Osborne & Rubinstein — decision/game-theoretic foundations.
* Hurwicz, Maskin, Myerson; Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences 2007 — mechanism design and incentive compatibility.
* Holmström; Hart; Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences 2016 — incentives, contracts, and principal–agent problems.
* Olson; Ostrom; Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences 2009 — collective action, commons, and institutional governance.
* World Bank governance literature — broad institutional/process framing of governance.
* Resnick et al. — online reputation systems.
* Douceur (2002) — Sybil attack.

== Cross-wave links ==

* [[Probability]], [[Conditional probability]], [[Bayesian inference]], and [[Uncertainty]] ground decision models.
